# Dev guidelines

How to navigate and extend this codebase. A fresh contributor should read this
file before adding code, so that new contributions land where the rest of the
project would expect them. This file is jointly maintained by `/refactor`
(style + idioms + anti-patterns) and `/unspaghettify` (file roles + dependency
direction + placement); each command MERGES into its own sections without
clobbering the other's.

## Module roles (one sentence each)

Grouped by layer, top of the dependency graph last.

### Leaves — depend on nothing in-project

- `launch/utils.py` — domain-neutral helpers (formatting, sorting, parsing, host:port split, user prompts).
- `launch/paths.py` — every host + container path the launcher knows; the *only* place paths get defined. Constants + path-builder lambdas only — no disk listings (those are file_access work).
- `launch/template_code/` — pure data, no logic: docker-side strings (`docker_prompts.py`), the curated firewall domain list (`firewall_domains.py` — CDN provider ranges are NOT data here or anywhere in source; network.py fetches them live).

### Disk — depends only on leaves

- `launch/file_access.py` — sole chokepoint for every disk-touching call (reads, atomic writes, removes, queries, lists, stats) — including the agents/ name → md-path index (`agent_md_index`, cached).

### The tag system — `launch/tags/`, depends on disk + leaves

- `tags/base.py` — the `Tag` record, `DockerContribution`, `tag.info`/`tag.docker` parsing, the STRICT tree rule, `TagError`.
- `tags/engine.py`, `tags/profession.py`, `tags/specialty.py`, `tags/policy.py` — the four kind classes, each with its own `scan(agents_dir)`; engine also owns the model-family sort keys (`engine_sort_key`), profession discovers hidden `_<name>` image layers, specialty adds `combos.info`, policy adds `merge_fragments`.
- `tags/registry.py` — `scan_all(agents_dir)` → `Registry`: discover, cross-validate (fail loud), look up.
- `tags/lego.py` — `AgentBuild` + `.lego` loading (an agent's default tag selections).
- `tags/identity.py` — `Agent` (pickable) and `Instance` (fully-resolved launch: `chain`, `build_steps`, `docker_contributions`, `conf`, `claude_args`, `unmet_wants`).
- `tags/store.py` — `instances.toml` load/save (stdlib `tomllib` in; a small fixed-schema TOML emitter out).
- `tags/migrations.py` — ISOLATED one-shot conversions from retired on-disk formats; the only module allowed to know retired formats exist.
- `tags/addendums.py` — the CLAUDE.md addendum composer: universal BASE_ADDENDUMS, the placeholder registry, and `compose(active_tags)`; per-tag copy lives in each tag.info's `[addendum]` table.

### Small services — depend on disk + tags

- `launch/claude_code_config.py` — Claude-Code-side display setup the launcher stages from the host (status line + terminal title).
- `launch/network.py` — `{firewall}` coordination: whitelist expansion (wildcard + IPv6-skip aware) + CDN-range widening, host-side DNS cascade, cross-launch IP cache (fresh-DNS union / failure fallback — never a substitute for a live lookup), burst-batched iptables updater (gated on init-firewall.sh's completion marker so it never races the in-container setup), and the post-launch refresher daemon that re-resolves the whitelist every few minutes to heal DNS drift. Critical Anthropic pins are widened to the registered `160.79.104.0/21` block, and `selftest_address()` feeds init-firewall.sh's DNS-free self-test (`$1` → `curl --resolve`); the script also probes/reorders dead-first nameservers in resolv.conf at startup.

### Orchestration — depend on services

- `launch/container_env.py` — env staging (`ContainerEnvKey` taxonomy, the staged-value accumulator, `-e`/build-arg formatters, `set_container_env` orchestrator).
- `launch/docker_config.py` — plain-docker orchestration: `ensure_image` (base + per-layer `docker build`), `run_container` (`docker run` assembly), tag.docker flag emitters, bind-mount accumulator, docker CLI wrappers, the dry-run gate.
- `launch/tag_handlers.py` — `apply_tags(instance)`: stages declarative tag.docker mounts, then dispatches per-tag `_apply_<name>` handlers (dynamic-only side effects; data-only tags have no handler).
- `launch/agents_crud.py` — instance state CRUD (instances.toml writers), state-dir installs (`install_latest_md`, `install_settings`), `resolve_pick`, picker-entry factories.
- `launch/user_additions.py` — user-side contributions to the container (optional-creds mounts + first-launch template plants).
- `launch/audit.py` — read-only state-correctness checker, runnable as `python -m launch.audit`.

### UI

- `launch/tag_form.py` — the kind-sectioned tag form (`prompt_tags` / `checkbox_form`: engine radio, requires-cascade, combos + wants warnings, context preamble) plus the shared TUI style system (`UiClass`, `tag_style`, the stance colors) both surfaces use.
- `launch/menu_picker.py` — prompt_toolkit picker + F8 legend + line prompts (workspace, session) + pre-launch banner; imports the form and styles from tag_form (one-way).

### Entry

- `run.py` — staged `launch()` orchestrator (scan tags → migrate store → input → fs-validate → resume → persist → apply tags → setup → build → run).

### Other directories

- `launch/template_files/` — source-side files `file_access.copy_file` plants to `user_extras/` on first launch.
- `launch/tests/` — unittest suite (`python3 -m unittest discover -s launch/tests` from project root).
- `agents/` — agent personas (`<name>.md` + `<name>.lego`) and the tag tree (`engine/`, `profession/`, `specialty/`, `policy/`).
- `Dockerfile` (repo root) — the base image; per-layer Dockerfiles live in the tag tree next to their tags.
- `settings/` — in-container bashrc + status line + manifest helpers + base Claude settings/keybindings (bind-mounted via `DOCKER_BASE_MOUNTS`; `settings.json` is merged with policy fragments per instance rather than mounted directly).
- `custom_commands/` — launcher-bundled slash commands (bind-mounted into every container).
- `custom_skills/` — launcher-bundled skills (bind-mounted as a single dir; each `<name>/SKILL.md` becomes a `/<name>` skill).

## Dependency direction

The arrows must stay one-way. A reversal creates a cycle (or hides one behind
lazy imports, which is the bandage of last resort).

```
leaves: utils, paths, template_code/   (independent — none imports in-project)
                  │
                  ▼
            file_access ──► tags/ ──┐
                                    ├─► small services (claude_code_config, network)
                                    │
                  small services ──► orchestration (container_env, docker_config, tag_handlers,
                                                    agents_crud, user_additions, audit)
                                             │
                                             ▼
                                             UI (tag_form ─► menu_picker)
                                             │
                                             ▼
                                             entry (run.py)
```

**One acknowledged cycle:** `docker_config ↔ network`. `docker_config` imports
`network` at module level (for `is_critical_pending` / `start_firewall_updater` /
`wait_for_critical_addresses`); `network._updater_worker` and
`network._insert_iptables_accept` lazy-import `docker_config.wait_for_container_running`
and `docker_config.docker_exec_root_subprocess` from inside the function body. Both
docstrings call this out. Don't add new lazy imports without good reason —
prefer to push the shared logic to a leaf-er module first.

## Where new code goes — by example

For each common addition, place it where the rest of the project would expect.
"X belongs in Y because Z" — the *because* matters more than the rule.

### A new tag (any kind)

→ a folder in the `agents/` tree — no launcher code:

- **Engine**: `agents/engine/<name>/{tag.info, engine.conf}`. Nested folders
  overlay the parent's conf key-by-key.
- **Profession**: `agents/profession/<name>/{tag.info, Dockerfile}` (+
  `tag.docker` naming the build-args the Dockerfile consumes). Nest under
  another profession to declare a requirement (`code/web/` ⇒ web requires code).
- **Specialty**: `agents/specialty/<name>/tag.info` (`short_description`,
  `full_description`, `warn`, `claude_args`, `workspace_readonly`, `[wants]`,
  `[addendum]`). May claim two hidden same-named assets: a `_<name>` image
  layer in the profession tree (→ `requires` + Dockerfile) and/or a
  `policy/_<name>/policy.json` settings fragment (merged like a policy). See
  `{ro}` for both-flavored (mount + fragment); `{dood}` for the layer. Image layer, if any: a hidden
  `_<name>/Dockerfile` under the profession it depends on. Static container
  config (mounts, `cap_add`, `entrypoint`, `env_forward`) goes in `tag.docker`.
  Multi-tag warnings go in `agents/specialty/combos.info`.
- **Policy**: `agents/policy/<name>/{tag.info, policy.json}` — a Claude Code
  settings fragment, merged per instance and mounted read-only. `stance`
  (`allow`/`deny`/`demand`) drives the orange/blue/white coloring; the
  shortname convention mirrors it (`+` grants, `-` restricts, `!` demands).

Only add an `_apply_<name>` handler in `tag_handlers.py` when the tag needs
*dynamic* host-side work (cache pruning, GID detection, DNS kickoff) that
declarative `tag.docker` data can't express; tags without a handler are
legal no-ops. If the tag should inject launch-time CLAUDE.md guidance, add
an `Addendum` in `tags/addendums.py` and key it under the tag's name in
`ADDENDUMS_BY_TAG`.

### A new host or container path

→ `paths.py`. Because every path constant in the project lives here — search
finds it once, edit-via-relocation is one line. Static composite paths go
inline with their constituents; dynamic paths get a `<name>_path` lambda at
the bottom.

### A new bind-mount

→ `DOCKER_BASE_MOUNTS` in `paths.py` when every launch needs it; the owning
tag's `tag.docker` `[run] mounts` when it belongs to a tag (that's data, not
code — `apply_tags` stages every contribution generically); `CACHE_MOUNTS`
for `[code]` cache shares.

### A new disk-touching helper

→ `file_access.py`. Because that's the project's chokepoint — no other module
calls `open()`, `Path.exists()`, `shutil.*`, etc. The chokepoint rule is what
makes the rest of the codebase mock-able and keeps OS-quirk handling
(root-owned bind-mount leftovers, atomic writes) in one place.

### A new env var staged for the container

→ `container_env.py`. If it's a known static key, add it to the
`ContainerEnvKey` enum (flag `container_emit=True` for unconditional `-e`
emission) and stage via `stage_container_env(key, value)`. Build-args are
pulled by each layer's `tag.docker` `[build] arg_forward`; tag-conditional
run env by `[run] env_forward` — so a tag-scoped var usually needs the enum
entry + one line in the owning `tag.docker`, not new emission code. Dynamic
INSTALL_<TOOL> / token vars derive from `OPTIONAL_CREDS_MOUNTS` and
`OPTIONAL_CREDS_TOKEN_ENV_VARS` in `paths.py`.

### A new directory-contents lookup (like the agents/ name → md index)

→ `file_access.py`, as a cached function (see `agent_md_index`). Because a
directory *listing* is disk I/O — the chokepoint rule — even when the result
feels constant-like; `paths.py` stays declarations-only.

### A new launch-time CLAUDE.md directive

→ the owning tag's `tag.info`, as an `[addendum]` table (`title` + `body`);
`compose(active_tags)` renders it as a `### <title>` section while the tag
is active. Bodies may use the `{placeholder}` names published in
`tags/addendums.py` (validated at scan). A launcher-universal notice (no
owning tag) goes in `BASE_ADDENDUMS` in that module instead.

### A new picker row kind

→ `menu_picker.py`. Define a dataclass for the row's data (see `ContEntry` /
`PickerEntry`). Add a `PickerRowMarker` enum member if you want a new glyph
prefix. Build entries via the existing `pick_with_preview` primitive — it's
generic over `PickerEntry.value`.

### A new state-correctness check

→ `audit.py`. Add a check function (the existing `_store_entry_issues`,
`_check_json_file` are unit-testable in isolation) and call it from `main()`.
Tests go in `launch/tests/test_audit.py`.

### A new identity property derived from existing fields

→ a `@property` on `Instance` (or `Agent`) in `tags/identity.py`. Don't add
a free function elsewhere — the dataclass owns its derivations. Same for any
"compute X from the instance" — it's `inst.x`, not `compute_x(inst)`.

### A new store-format change

→ the format itself lives in `tags/store.py`; the conversion FROM the
previous format goes in `tags/migrations.py` (the only module that may know
retired formats exist) as an extension of `ensure_migrated`.

### A new helper that's pure (no disk, no third-party)

→ `utils.py`. Domain-neutral helpers only. If it needs disk, it goes in
`file_access.py`. If it needs an identity dataclass, it's a property /
method on that class.

## Anti-patterns

Things to NOT do, with the reason:

- **Don't import `Path` or call `open()` outside `file_access.py`.** The
  chokepoint rule is what keeps disk-quirk handling (sudo fallbacks, atomic
  writes) in one place. New disk reader → new function in `file_access.py`.
- **Don't add a constant `FOO_PATH` outside `paths.py`.** Search returns one
  hit for any path; that's the point.
- **Don't hard-code a tag name in launcher logic when the tag tree can carry
  the fact.** Requirements come from tree nesting; container config from
  `tag.docker`; copy from `tag.info`. An `if name == "code"` in orchestration
  is a smell — the handful of legitimate ones (cache mounts, the whitelist
  plant) live in `tag_handlers` / `user_additions` and say why.
- **Don't add a free function that takes an `Instance` and computes one of
  its fields.** That's a `@property` on the dataclass — same call site
  shape, lives next to the rest of the identity surface.
- **Don't teach any module outside `tags/migrations.py` about retired
  formats.** Format knowledge ages out in one place.
- **Don't introduce a new cycle (even a lazy one).** The existing `network
  ↔ docker_config` cycle is a documented exception; new ones get pushed to
  a leaf-er module first.
- **Don't add a `# noqa` without naming the rule.** `# noqa: E402` not bare
  `# noqa`. Same for `# type: ignore[misc]` over bare `# type: ignore`.
- **Don't re-export by importing-and-not-using.** If module A needs `X` and
  `X` lives in module C, import directly: `from .C import X`. Routing it
  through B (`from .B import X`) is a re-export hop with no value.
- **Don't write raw `subprocess.run([cmd, ...], capture_output=True, text=True)`.**
  Use `utils.shell_capture(*cmd)` — same shape, less noise, signals "this is a
  read-only probe of a CLI tool." Every read-only docker/getent probe routes
  through it. Reserve direct `subprocess.run` / `subprocess.call` for cases
  that genuinely need extra kwargs (`timeout=`, `env=`, `check=True`).
- **Functions that exist to prompt the user start with `prompt_`.** `prompt_keypress`,
  `prompt_install_failures`, `prompt_tags`, `prompt_session` — name reflects
  the side-effect shape. Don't pick a name that reads like a getter
  (`collect_*`, `check_*`, `get_*`) when the function returns `None` and
  exists for the user-facing prompt.
